\sender{Alex Example}{Brisbane, QLD}{alex@example.com \textperiodcentered\ github.com/example}
\recipient{Hiring Manager \\ Example Pty Ltd}
\letterdate{12 November 2026}
\subject{Application: Field Systems Technician (REQ-1234)}
\begin{document}
\begin{jpletter}{Dear Hiring Manager,}
I am applying for the Field Systems Technician role. I bring decades of hands-on Linux, networking and hardware experience, backed by open-source integrations I publish and maintain.

In my current role I work to strict procedures in a high-throughput environment; outside work I run a segmented UniFi network, self-hosted services and a household NixOS fleet. I would welcome the chance to bring the same care to your field team.

Thank you for considering my application. My resume is attached.
\end{jpletter}
\signoff{Kind regards,}{Alex Example}
\end{document}

\documentclass{jupiter-notes}
\title{Network migration notes}
\notemeta{2026-11-12}{infra, unifi}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section{Plan}
Move the camera VLAN behind its own firewall zone before touching the IoT network.\jpmargin{Check the UDM Pro firmware first.}

\begin{jptodo}
  \todo{Create the camera zone and block inter-VLAN traffic}
  \todo{Re-adopt the three access points}
  \done{Back up the controller}
\end{jptodo}

\section{Findings}
\subsection{Switch ports}
Port profiles are inherited from the VLAN, so overrides need to be cleared first.\jpmargin{Profiles live under Settings $\rightarrow$ Profiles.}
\begin{jpnote}[warn]Do not change the native VLAN while connected over it.\end{jpnote}

